From 6f50b90b3ec9de8472a557a4929e3b5ad4df98f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florent Merlet <florentmerlet73@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:10:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] TIP 759 (5/7): tuple assignment (a, (b, c)) = list

In an expression, the target of "=" may be a list of names, nested at
will: ((x0, y0), (x1, y1)) = ($p, $q). Each variable gets the element
of the value at the same place (lindex semantics: "" if missing). As for
"=", a name is a bareword, or any operand whose value is the name.

- ParseExpr: FindTupleTarget finds a parenthesized group followed by "=";
  its barewords that are not function calls are names.
- CompileExprTree: the paths of the names are a literal list; when every
  name is a compiled local, the list of names is not built at run time.
- New instruction tupleAssign (INST_TUPLE_ASSIGN), TupleAssign in
  tclExecute.c: stores by local index, or by name.
- Tests: exprTuple.test; exprAssign-5.6. Docs: expr.n.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MrNWAEyXLqekovNBvbMZ4z
